.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt PATINA-COMPOSITOR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm patina-compositor
.Nd the Patina desktop's Wayland compositor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -session
.Op Ar command ...
.Nm
.Fl h | V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a floating Wayland compositor: it owns the screen, the keyboard and
the pointer, places and draws windows, and starts the rest of the desktop
as its clients.
.Pp
It runs one of two ways, chosen by whether
.Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set when it starts:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nested
As a window on another desktop (GNOME, sway, dwl), drawn with OpenGL ES,
or in software where there is none.
It quits when that desktop goes.
Its D-Bus names can't be served where another
.Nm
already serves them, so nested on a Patina session it wants a session bus
of its own:
.Dl dbus-run-session -- patina-compositor
.It On the hardware
From a text console or the login screen: it takes the GPU and the input
devices through the login session, and shows every connected monitor, the
laptop's panel first, extended or mirrored as patina-displays sets it.
.El
.Pp
At start-up it runs Xwayland, when installed, for X11 apps.
An X11 window that draws no frame of its own, and a Wayland window that asks
for one through xdg-decoration, gets a title bar from the
compositor, in the bar's skin: drag it to move the window, double-click it
or use its button to maximise, its other buttons to minimise and close, and
drag the edges to resize.
A window that asks for a shadow through KDE's shadow protocol, as Patina apps do,
has it drawn under it.
It also runs
.Xr patina-wallpaper 1 ,
.Xr patina-hud 1
and
.Xr patina-tiler 1 ;
then each
.Ar command
given.
Its keys run the launcher, the desktop's menu, the lock screen and the
volume flash, as configured below.
Every program it starts is listed with its output on Settings' Processes
page
.Pq Xr patina-settings 1 .
The wallpaper, the bar and the tiler are started again when they exit,
unless they exit five times within thirty seconds.
.Pp
Windows float: each is placed once, cascading beside the bar, and a click
focuses and raises it.
How windows tile is
.Xr patina-tiler 1 Ns 's
to decide: the compositor reports drags and Super with an arrow over the
session bus, and places windows where it's told.
Dragging a tiled window away restores it.
Where tiled windows meet side by side, the gap between them (or, without
gaps, the few pixels either side of where they touch) shows a left-right
arrow: dragging it grows the windows on one side as it shrinks those on the
other, keeping the gap, and the tiler keeps the new split.
Only the app's own maximise button (or a double click on its title bar)
maximises a window, edge to edge.
With gaps on, it fills the screen inside the gaps instead, as Super+Up does,
and keeps its rounded corners; the app's maximise again restores it.
.Ic maximise_inside
under
.Ic [gaps]
turns that off.
The bar's tabs are the list of windows, and Alt+Tab goes by most recent use.
Closing a window gives the keyboard back to the window it came from, or,
if that's gone, focuses its neighbouring tab.
A minimised window leaves the screen and keeps its tab; focusing it again,
by its tab, Alt+Tab or Super+1 to 9, puts it back where it was.
The apps pinned in the bar's config come first, and dragging a tab
moves its window along the others
.Pq Sy com.sectorflabs.Patina.Tabs ,
which the keys follow.
A window asking for fullscreen covers the whole screen, over the bar.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -session
Run as a login session: export
.Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.Ev DISPLAY
and
.Ev XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
to systemd and D-Bus, so services started later, such as
xdg-desktop-portal, reach this desktop.
On the hardware this is done anyway.
.Xr patina-session 1
passes it on from the login screen's entry.
.It Fl h , -help
Print a short usage message.
.It Fl V , -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh KEYS
The defaults.
.Ic [keys]
in the config changes all but Alt+Tab, the process manager, quitting,
restarting, the consoles and the media keys, within a second of a save.
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Backspace"
.It Super
Open the launcher as its button in the HUD does, from the button's place:
Super tapped on its own.
.It Super+Space
Open the launcher as a search box at the top of the middle of the screen,
as Spotlight does on a Mac.
.It Super+H
Hide the HUD, or bring it back:
.Ql patina-hud --toggle .
.It Super+1 No to Sy 9
Bring forward the window in that tab of the bar, or start the pinned app
whose tab it is.
.It Alt+Tab , Alt+Shift+Tab
Go to the window used before this one: a quick press swaps with it, and again swaps back.
Holding Alt and pressing Tab again goes further back; Shift goes the other way.
.It Ctrl+Shift+Escape
Open the process manager,
.Xr patina-processes 1 ,
or bring its window forward.
.It Super+Q
Quit the focused app: ask all its windows to close.
.It Super+M
Minimise the focused window to its tab.
.It Super+Up , Super+Down
Fill the screen with the focused window, tiled, and restore it; also
Ctrl+Super+K and Ctrl+Super+J.
.It Super+Left , Super+Right
Fill the left or right half of the screen; also Ctrl+Super+H and
Ctrl+Super+L.
These four are
.Xr patina-tiler 1 Ns 's
to decide, and do as it says.
.It Super+Shift+ Ns Ar arrow
Grow the focused window that way by a tenth of the screen.
.It Ctrl+Super+Shift+K , Ctrl+Super+Shift+J
Grow and shrink the focused window around its centre.
.It Ctrl+Super+Shift+L , Ctrl+Super+Shift+H
Widen and narrow it.
.It Super+Escape , Ctrl+Super+Q
Lock the screen.
.It Super+Shift+3
Save a screenshot of the desktop in
.Pa ~/Pictures/Screenshots .
.It Super+Shift+4
Save a screenshot of the focused window.
Every screenshot is also put on the clipboard, unless
.Ic [clipboard]
turns that off.
.It Print , Super+Shift+5
Offer the desktop, the focused window or a selection dragged out with the
pointer, or recording the desktop or a selection, with
.Xr patina-screenshot 1 .
While recording, the key stops it.
.It Ctrl+Super+Space , Super+period
Open the emoji picker,
.Xr patina-emoji 1 ,
as Ctrl+Cmd+Space does on a Mac.
.It Ctrl+Super+P , XF86Display
Open the monitors' popup,
.Xr patina-displays 1 :
extend the desktop across the monitors or mirror it.
It also opens by itself when a monitor is plugged in that it must ask about: a
new one, or one whose saved display asks.
A monitor it knows is switched on as the desktop was last used.
.It Ctrl+Space
Switch to the next keyboard layout, as on a Mac.
.It Ctrl+Super+Alt
Held, dictate: what's said while it's down is typed into the focused
window when it comes up, by
.Xr patina-dictate 1 .
.It Super+Shift+R
Read the config file again.
.It Super+ Ns Ar key
Any other key with Super, or Super+Shift, goes to the focused window
with Ctrl in place of Super, as Cmd does on a Mac: Super+T opens a tab,
Super+W closes one, Super+C and Super+V copy and paste.
A terminal, whose desktop entry lists
.Cm TerminalEmulator
in its Categories, gets Ctrl+Shift instead, and Ctrl+Alt+Shift for
Super+Shift, so Super+D and Super+Shift+D stay apart there too.
Not while the screen is locked or a fullscreen window has the keyboard.
.It Ctrl+Alt+F1 No to Sy F12
Switch to that console, on the hardware.
.It Ctrl+Alt+Backspace
Quit, ending the session: the way out whatever state it's in.
.It Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Backspace
Restart the desktop without logging out, under
.Xr patina-session 1 .
Every window closes.
Refused while the screen is locked.
.El
.Pp
The brightness and volume keys change them, and
.Xr patina-volume 1
flashes up to show the level.
Super and a drag moves any window.
A right click on the empty desktop opens
.Xr patina-menu 1 .
On a touchpad, three fingers drag.
A touchscreen's touches go to the app under them, which handles them if it
handles touch at all; a touch focuses and raises the window it lands on.
The desktop's own pieces and window frames don't answer to touch yet.
While the screen is locked, only the console and media keys work.
While a fullscreen window has the keyboard, as a game does, its keys go to
it: only Alt+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Escape, Ctrl+Alt+Backspace,
Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Backspace, the console and the media keys still work, and tapping Super doesn't open the
launcher.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The settings are in
.Pa ~/.config/patina-compositor/config.toml ,
written on first start with every setting commented out at its default.
Every key is optional.
Saving the file applies it within a second; mistakes are reported in the
log and never stop the desktop.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic scale
The screen's scale, such as 1.5.
Unset, it's chosen from the panel's density, or the parent desktop's when
nested.
.It Ic launcher
What tapping Super opens;
.Dq patina-launcher
by default, and
.Dq
for nothing.
It is run by
.Ql patina-hud --open launcher ,
which gives it where the HUD's button is.
.It Ic launcher_spotlight
What Super+Space runs;
.Dq patina-launcher --position center --search top
by default, and
.Dq
for nothing.
.It Ic desktop_menu
What a right click on the desktop runs, given
.Fl -at Ar x , Ns Ar y ;
.Dq patina-menu
by default.
.It Ic [keyboard]
.Ic layout , variant , model
and
.Ic options
as in XKB, from
.Pa /etc/default/keyboard
when unset, the layouts as a list Ctrl+Space steps through,
.Dq us,se ,
four at most: XKB leaves out any after the fourth, which the log names;
.Ic repeat_delay
(milliseconds) and
.Ic repeat_rate
(per second);
.Ic caps_lock :
.Dq caps_lock ,
.Dq escape ,
.Dq ctrl
or
.Dq swap_escape ;
.Ic caps_hold :
.Dq none ,
or
.Dq layer ,
Caps Lock held sending the keys of
.Ic [caps_layer] ,
which leaves
.Ic caps_lock
out; and
.Ic caps_tap ,
what Caps Lock tapped alone does when it's a layer:
.Dq caps_lock ,
.Dq escape
or
.Dq none .
Modifiers are swapped with
.Ic options ,
so the desktop's own keys see them swapped too:
.Dq altwin:swap_alt_win
swaps Alt and Super,
.Dq altwin:swap_lalt_lwin
only the left ones,
.Dq altwin:ctrl_win
makes Super Ctrl and
.Dq ctrl:swap_lwin_lctl
swaps left Super and Ctrl.
Keys are named by what they are after the swap.
.It Ic [touchpad]
.Ic tap
to click,
.Ic natural_scroll ,
.Ic speed
from -1.0 to 1.0,
.Ic three_finger_drag ,
and
.Ic four_finger_swipe ,
which minimises every window on four fingers toward the edge the HUD's window list is on, and brings them back on four away from it.
.It Ic [cursor]
.Ic theme ,
an installed cursor theme instead of Patina's own, and
.Ic size
in pixels at scale 1.
.It Ic [media]
.Ic brightness_step
in percent;
.Ic volume_up , volume_down , mute
and
.Ic mic_mute ,
commands run through
.Xr sh 1
.Pq Xr wpctl 1 No by default ;
and
.Ic volume_menu ,
what flashes up after a volume key.
.It Ic [lock]
.Ic command ,
the lock screen
.Pq Dq patina-lock ,
any ext-session-lock locker;
.Ic on_suspend ,
whether to lock before the machine sleeps; and
.Ic on_screen_off ,
whether to lock when the screen turns off, as it does by default, on the
hardware.
.It Ic [screen]
.Ic off_after :
seconds without input before the screen turns off; 300 by default, 0
for never.
Any input turns it back on.
Something playing holds it on: a window using the idle-inhibit protocol,
or a holder of
.Sy org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver Ns 's
Inhibit, as Steam is while a game runs.
On the hardware the monitor is powered off; nested, it's only logged.
.It Ic [night_light]
.Ic mode :
.Dq off ,
.Dq always
or
.Dq schedule ;
.Ic from
and
.Ic to
as
.Dq HH:MM ;
and
.Ic temperature
in kelvin, from 6500 (neutral) to 1700;
and
.Ic pause_in_fullscreen ,
which leaves the colours alone while a fullscreen window has the keyboard (off by default; ADR-0132).
On the hardware only.
.It Ic [gaps]
With
.Ic enabled
true, tiled windows (halves, and windows filling the screen) keep
.Ic size
logical pixels (8 by default, up to 64) around and between them, and
.Xr patina-hud 1
floats that far off the screen's edges with corners rounded by
.Ic radius .
Tiled windows keep their rounded corners, and an app's maximise fills
inside the gaps like Super+Up rather than edge to edge.
With
.Ic maximise_inside
false, the default being true, an app's maximise stays a real one, edge to
edge over the gaps.
Off by default.
.It Ic [shadows]
With
.Ic enabled
true, the default, the compositor draws the shadow a window asks for through
KDE's shadow protocol, as Patina apps and the HUD's parts do, lighter while a
window isn't focused.
Windows that draw their own, as Firefox does, keep it.
.It Ic [clipboard]
The clipboard history.
.Ic enabled
true, the default, keeps what is copied; false keeps nothing and drops what was kept.
.Ic screenshots
true, the default, puts every screenshot on the clipboard, whether or not a history is kept.
.Ic max_entries
is how many copies are kept, 50 by default, up to 200.
.Ic expire_after
is how many seconds a copy is kept for, a week by default; 0 keeps it until newer ones push it out.
A copy that expires while the compositor is serving it as the clipboard stops pasting too; an app's own is left alone.
.It Ic [bindings]
Keys that run commands, such as
.Dl \(dqAlt+T\(dq = \(dqfoot\(dq
Modifiers (Super, Alt, Ctrl, Shift) and a key by its name.
.It Ic [keys]
The desktop's own keys, by action: one key or a list each, written as for
.Ic [bindings] ,
with
.Dq Super
alone for Super tapped on its own, as
.Dl snap_right = [\(dqSuper+Right\(dq, \(dqCtrl+Super+L\(dq]
An action set here replaces its defaults, and the rest keep theirs;
.Dq
or [] takes its keys away, and a Super key no action has goes to the app as
Ctrl.
The actions are
.Ic launcher , launcher_spotlight , emoji , displays , next_layout , quit_app , minimise , lock ,
.Ic reload , screenshot , screenshot_window , screenshot_menu , dictate
(which may be modifiers alone, held, as
.Dq Ctrl+Super+Alt ) ,
.Ic snap_ Ns Ar side
and
.Ic grow_ Ns Ar side
(up, down, left, right),
.Ic resize_grow , resize_shrink , resize_wider , resize_narrower
and
.Ic window_1
to
.Ic window_9 ;
the config written on first start lists each with its keys.
An unknown action or key, or a key given two actions, is logged and left
out.
.It Ic [remap]
Keys sent to the focused window as others, both written as for
.Ic [bindings] ,
as
.Dl \(dqAlt+T\(dq = \(dqCtrl+T\(dq
The window is told only the remap's modifiers are down while it's held.
These add to the built-in ones, or replace one for the same key, and
.Dq
takes one away.
A key that is fixed or in
.Ic [keys]
can't be remapped; a remap wins over
.Ic [bindings]
and over Super as Ctrl.
.It Ic [caps_layer]
Keys sent as others while Caps Lock is held, with
.Ic caps_hold
.Dq layer :
one key alone, then what it sends, as
.Dl \(dqH\(dq = \(dqLeft\(dq
The modifiers held go with it, so Shift, Caps Lock and L is Shift+Right.
Caps Lock tapped alone, under 200 ms with no other key, does
.Ic caps_tap
when it comes up.
Off while locked and under a fullscreen game, where Caps Lock is itself.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Set: run nested in that desktop.
Unset: run on the hardware.
.It Ev PATINA_SCALE
The scale, over the config's.
.It Ev PATINA_RENDERER
.Dq pixman
draws in software, nested.
.It Ev PATINA_SESSION
Set by
.Xr patina-session 1 ,
which will start the compositor again: restarting is allowed, and a
screen locked before a crash starts locked.
.El
.Pp
Its clients get
.Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.Ev DISPLAY
for Xwayland,
.Ev XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP Ns =Patina ,
a
.Ev PATH
with the compositor's own folder first, and
.Ev TERMINAL Ns =teleprinter
where it's installed, unless
.Ev TERMINAL
is set already: the terminal apps open.
.Sh SESSION BUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Processes
What the compositor started: List, Restart, Stop, Log, Spawn, and
RestartDesktop with the Supervised property.
For
.Xr patina-settings 1 ,
and for
.Xr patina-hud 1
and
.Xr patina-menu 1 ,
which start what they open through Spawn.
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Session
Lock, which runs the
.Ic [lock] command ,
and LogOut, which quits cleanly; for
.Xr patina-power 1 .
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.NightLight
Night Light's state, overrides and inhibitors, on the hardware; see
.Xr patina-night-light 1 .
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Displays
The monitors: List, Mode and SetMode (extend, mirror or off), SetPlace (which side
of which monitor each sits on), Saved, SetAsk and Forget (the displays remembered), and
the signals Changed and MonitorAdded; for
.Xr patina-displays 1
and
.Xr patina-settings 1 .
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Keyboard
The keymap's layouts (Layouts: each one's XKB layout, variant and name)
and the one in use (Current), announced as they change, and SetCurrent;
for the layout in
.Xr patina-hud 1 Ns 's
tray and
.Xr patina-settings 1 .
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Clipboard
The clipboard's history: List (each copy's id, kind, a short preview,
thumbnail and time, newest first), Pick, Delete and Clear, Changed
signalled when it changes, and Enabled and Paused, which can be set.
Copies are read out of the app that made them and kept, so one survives
its app quitting; those a password manager marks private are left out.
Wayland windows only.
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Input
Commit, which types a text into the focused window: through the
clipboard, with Ctrl+V, or Ctrl+Shift+V in a terminal, once modifiers
held by hand are let go; the clipboard is given back as it was after.
Wayland windows only.
And DictationStarted and DictationStopped, signalled as the
.Ic dictate
key goes down and comes up.
And FullscreenChanged, with the property Fullscreen: whether a fullscreen window
has the keyboard, a game or a video.
For
.Xr patina-dictate 1 ,
which the compositor starts and keeps running.
.It org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver
Inhibit and UnInhibit, which hold the screen on until let go of or until
the caller leaves the bus; SimulateUserActivity, as if there were input;
and GetActive, whether the screen is off.
Not served where another desktop already has the name.
.It com.sectorflabs.Patina.Tiling
Drags, Super with an arrow, the work area, and Resized for each tiled
window after the line between tiled windows is dragged, as signals; Windows,
WorkArea, Gap, Screen, Place, Restore, ShowPreview and HidePreview.
With gaps on, WorkArea is already inside the outer gaps, and Gap is the
gap to leave between windows.
The owner of
.Sy com.sectorflabs.Patina.Tiler
decides how dragged windows tile and what Super with an arrow does:
.Xr patina-tiler 1 ,
or a program that takes the name from it until it leaves the bus.
The compositor only reports them and places windows where it's told; with
no tiler, windows don't tile.
.El
.Sh SCREEN CAPTURE
Other programs may capture the screen, or one window, through the
ext-image-copy-capture-v1 protocol, with the windows named by
ext-foreign-toplevel-list-v1.
.Xr xdg-desktop-portal-wlr 5
is the client that matters: OBS and browsers sharing the screen ask the
portal, which asks the compositor, with
.Nm patina-screenshot Fl -share
choosing what's shared.
A frame is sent once something on screen has changed.
Screen recordings from the Print key are drawn by the compositor and
encoded by
.Xr ffmpeg 1 ,
into
.Pa ~/Videos/Screen Recordings .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/patina-compositor/config.toml
The settings above.
.It Pa ~/.config/patina-compositor/displays.toml
Whether the desktop extends across several monitors or mirrors, and where each
monitor sits, by monitor: one placed before goes where it was, and one of the
same model plugged in later goes where a monitor of that model was put.
The compositor writes it; the Displays page and
.Xr patina-displays 1
change it through the bus.
.It Pa ~/Pictures/Screenshots/
Screenshots.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when it quits, 75 when it quits to be started again by
.Xr patina-session 1 ,
and 1 on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Try it as a window on the current desktop, with a terminal:
.Pp
.Dl $ patina-compositor foot
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr patina-dictate 1 ,
.Xr patina-hud 1 ,
.Xr patina-session 1 ,
.Xr patina-settings 1 ,
.Xr patina-tiler 1 ,
.Xr patina-desktop 7
